# Normalize line endings so a Windows checkout of the public Strixgov/strix
# repo doesn't rewrite every file to CRLF on the next `git add`. Text is stored
# LF in the repo; Git handles the working-copy conversion.
#
# Sibling of release/strixgov-skills/.gitattributes, which got this treatment
# after "the churn seen during the 2026-07 manual sync". This tree did not, and
# the 2026-08-05 operator push surfaced the consequence: every `git add` printed
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" across the mirror,
# including the vendored verifier.
#
# The sharper reason, specific to this tree: plugins/strix-verifier/vendor/
# PROVENANCE.json pins `vendoredTreeSha256`, a digest over the vendored file
# CONTENTS. Any path that lands CRLF in that tree breaks the digest for every
# public consumer re-verifying the plugin — a silent trust-artifact failure, not
# just cosmetic churn. Keeping the mirror LF-only removes that failure mode by
# construction.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf

# Executable entry points must stay LF or they fail to run under sh on
# Linux/macOS (`#!/usr/bin/env node` followed by CR is not a valid interpreter).
plugins/strix-verifier/bin/strix-verify              text eol=lf
plugins/strix-verifier/vendor/strixgov-verifier/bin/verify.mjs text eol=lf

# The Windows launcher is the one deliberate exception: .cmd is parsed by
# cmd.exe, which wants CRLF.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
